@startuml C4_Supplementary_Vertical_Slice_Pattern
title Supplementary: Vertical Slice vs Layered Architecture — Pattern Comparison

' ============================================================
' SUPPLEMENTARY DIAGRAM: VERTICAL SLICE PATTERN EXPLAINED
' ============================================================
' This diagram contrasts the vertical slice architecture used
' in this project with a traditional layered (horizontal)
' architecture, showing why the vertical slice approach was
' chosen for the Commission Calculator.
'
' PURPOSE:
' Explain the architectural pattern choice and how code is
' organized by FEATURE rather than by TECHNICAL LAYER.
' ============================================================

skinparam packageFontSize 12
skinparam noteFontSize 10

' ============================================================
' TRADITIONAL LAYERED ARCHITECTURE (for comparison)
' ============================================================

package "Traditional Layered Architecture\n(NOT used here)" as layered {
    package "Controllers" as layCtrl {
        [DealController] as ldc
        [PlanController] as lpc
        [CalcController] as lcc
        [DisputeController] as ldic
    }
    package "Services" as laySvc {
        [DealService] as lds
        [PlanService] as lps
        [CalcService] as lcs
        [DisputeService] as ldis
    }
    package "Repositories" as layRepo {
        [DealRepo] as ldr
        [PlanRepo] as lpr
        [CalcRepo] as lcr
        [DisputeRepo] as ldir
    }

    ldc --> lds
    lpc --> lps
    lcc --> lcs
    ldic --> ldis
    lds --> ldr
    lps --> lpr
    lcs --> lcr
    ldis --> ldir
}

note bottom of layered
    **LAYERED (Horizontal Slices)**

    Organized by TECHNICAL CONCERN:
    all controllers together, all services
    together, all repositories together.

    PROBLEMS:
    - Adding a feature touches every layer
    - Changes ripple across packages
    - Hard to see feature boundaries
    - Tight coupling between layers
end note

' ============================================================
' VERTICAL SLICE ARCHITECTURE (used in this project)
' ============================================================

package "Vertical Slice Architecture\n(USED in this project)" as vertical {

    package "features/deals/" as vDeals {
        [DealController] as vdc
        [DealService] as vds
        [DealRepository] as vdr
        [CreateDealRequest] as vdcr
        [DealResponse] as vdresp
        vdc --> vds
        vds --> vdr
    }

    package "features/plans/" as vPlans {
        [PlanController] as vpc
        [PlanService] as vps
        [PlanRepository] as vpr
        [CreatePlanRequest] as vpcr
        [PlanResponse] as vpresp
        vpc --> vps
        vps --> vpr
    }

    package "features/calculations/" as vCalcs {
        [CalcController] as vcc
        [CalcService] as vcs
        [CalcRepository] as vcr
        [CalcRequest] as vccr
        [CalcResponse] as vcresp
        vcc --> vcs
        vcs --> vcr
    }

    package "features/disputes/" as vDisputes {
        [DisputeController] as vdic
        [DisputeService] as vdis
        [DisputeRepository] as vdir
        [DisputeRequest] as vdicr
        [DisputeResponse] as vdiresp
        vdic --> vdis
        vdis --> vdir
    }

    package "domain/" as vDomain {
        [Shared Entities] as entities
    }

    package "infrastructure/" as vInfra {
        [Config + Exceptions] as infra
    }

    vdr --> entities
    vpr --> entities
    vcr --> entities
    vdir --> entities
}

note bottom of vertical
    **VERTICAL SLICES (Feature-based)**

    Organized by BUSINESS CAPABILITY:
    each feature owns its full stack
    (Controller + Service + Repo + DTOs).

    BENEFITS:
    - Adding a feature = one new package
    - Changes are contained within a slice
    - Clear feature boundaries
    - Slices can evolve independently
    - Easy to understand and navigate
    - Natural mapping to MCP tools

    SHARED CONCERNS:
    - Domain entities (shared, read-only)
    - Infrastructure (security, exceptions)
end note

' ============================================================
' PROJECT DIRECTORY MAPPING
' ============================================================

note as dirNote
    **PROJECT DIRECTORY STRUCTURE**

    architecture/verticalslice/
    ├── CommissionCalculatorApplication.java
    ├── domain/                    ← Shared entities (23 classes)
    │   ├── User.java
    │   ├── Deal.java, DealProduct.java
    │   ├── CommissionPlan.java, CommissionTier.java
    │   ├── CommissionRule.java, RuleCondition.java
    │   ├── CommissionCalculation.java
    │   ├── Dispute.java, DisputeComment.java
    │   └── (enums: UserRole, DealStatus, PlanStatus, ...)
    ├── features/                  ← 4 vertical slices
    │   ├── deals/                 ← Controller + Service + Repo + DTOs
    │   ├── plans/                 ← Controller + Service + Repo + DTOs
    │   ├── calculations/          ← Controller + Service + Repo + DTOs
    │   └── disputes/              ← Controller + Service + Repo + DTOs
    └── infrastructure/            ← Cross-cutting concerns
        ├── config/                ← SecurityConfig, OpenApiConfig
        ├── data/                  ← DataInitializer
        ├── exceptions/            ← GlobalExceptionHandler
        └── mcp/                   ← McpCommissionTools, McpPrompts, McpResources
end note

@enduml
